+# 2026-11-11 Does Node 22 global fetch() honour HTTPS_PROXY by default?
+
+## Answer
+
+**No.** In Node 22, global `fetch()` ignores `HTTPS_PROXY` / `HTTP_PROXY` / `NO_PROXY` by default.
+Since **v22.21.0** you can opt in by setting `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` or passing `--use-env-proxy`. Before v22.21.0, Node 22 has no built-in switch for this.
+
+## Findings
+
+| # | Claim | Source | Applies to | Basis |
+|---|-------|--------|------------|-------|
+| 1 | `--use-env-proxy` was "Added in: v22.21.0" and has Stability 1.1 (Active Development). The docs say: "When enabled, Node.js parses the `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables during startup, and tunnels requests over the specified proxy." | https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/cli.html#--use-env-proxy | v22.21.0+ | read |
+| 2 | `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` was "Added in: v22.21.0" and does the same thing. If both are set, `--use-env-proxy` takes precedence. | https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/cli.html#node_use_env_proxy1 | v22.21.0+ | read |
+| 3 | The "Built-in Proxy Support" section (Added in v22.21.0) says the global agent uses `proxyEnv: process.env` only "if the `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` environment variable is set to `1` or `--use-env-proxy` is enabled". So the proxy is off unless you turn it on. The default for `http.Agent`'s `proxyEnv` option is `undefined`. | https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/http.html#built-in-proxy-support | v22.21.0+ | read |
+| 4 | The v22 docs pages above describe the opt-in for `http`/`https` agents but do not explicitly mention `fetch()`. Item 5 shows the opt-in also covers `fetch()`. | same pages | v22.x docs | read |
+| 5 | On Node **v22.23.4**, a test with a local fake proxy gave these results:<br>• `HTTPS_PROXY` set alone: the proxy received **nothing**, and `fetch` tried DNS directly (`ENOTFOUND`).<br>• Adding `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`: the proxy received `CONNECT proxytest.invalid:443 HTTP/1.1`.<br>• Using `--use-env-proxy` instead: same `CONNECT` was received.<br>With the opt-in on, Node prints `[UNDICI-EHPA] Warning: EnvHttpProxyAgent is experimental`, so fetch is using undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent`. | test script below; local `node --version` = v22.23.4 | v22.23.4 | measured |
+| 6 | Node 22 versions before v22.21.0 have no built-in env-proxy support for `fetch`. The usual workaround there is to call undici's `setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent())` / `ProxyAgent` from the `undici` npm package. | inferred from the "Added in: v22.21.0" markers in items 1–3; not tested on an older 22.x | v22.0.0–v22.20.x | inferred |
+
+## How it was measured
+
+The test script was `proxytest.js`, kept in the session scratchpad and not committed. It:
+
+1. Starts a TCP listener on `127.0.0.1` as a fake proxy and records the first request line it receives.
+2. Spawns a child `node` with a minimal environment that calls `fetch('https://proxytest.invalid/')`.
+3. Runs the child three times: with `HTTPS_PROXY` alone, with `HTTPS_PROXY` plus `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`, and with `HTTPS_PROXY` plus `--use-env-proxy`.
+
+Output on v22.23.4:
+
+```
+HTTPS_PROXY only: proxy saw []; child: error ENOTFOUND
+HTTPS_PROXY + NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1: proxy saw ["CONNECT proxytest.invalid:443 HTTP/1.1"]; ... [UNDICI-EHPA] Warning: EnvHttpProxyAgent is experimental ...
+HTTPS_PROXY + --use-env-proxy: proxy saw ["CONNECT proxytest.invalid:443 HTTP/1.1"]; ... [UNDICI-EHPA] Warning: EnvHttpProxyAgent is experimental ...
+```
+
+## Open questions
+
+- I could not read the Node source (`lib/internal/process/pre_execution.js` at tag v22.21.0) for the exact code that wires `fetch` to `EnvHttpProxyAgent`, because the network fetch wasn't approved in this run. So the claim that the opt-in covers `fetch` rests on the measurement in item 5, not on the source or the docs text.
+- Pre-v22.21.0 behaviour (item 6) is inferred, not tested.
+- Both flags are Stability 1.1, so their behaviour may still change in later 22.x releases.
